To solve this problem, we need to determine the pressure at which $\mathrm{C}$ (graphite) is in equilibrium with $\mathrm{C}$ (diamond) at $298 \mathrm{~K}$.

Given:
- $\Delta_{f} G^{o}[\mathrm{C}(\text { graphite })]=0 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$
- $\Delta_{f} G^{o}[\mathrm{C}(\text { diamond })]=2.9 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$
- Volume change of $\mathrm{C}$ (graphite) to $\mathrm{C}$ (diamond) is $2 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{~m}^{3} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$

We can use the following equation to find the pressure at which $\mathrm{C}$ (graphite) is in equilibrium with $\mathrm{C}$ (diamond):

\[
\Delta_{f} G^{o}[\mathrm{C}(\text { graphite })] - \Delta_{f} G^{o}[\mathrm{C}(\text { diamond })] = -R \ln \left(\frac{\mathrm{P}_{\text {diamond }}}{\mathrm{P}_{\text {graphite }}}\right)
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
0 - 2.9 = -R \ln \left(\frac{\mathrm{P}_{\text {diamond }}}{\mathrm{P}_{\text {graphite }}}\right)
\]

